(1) Student 1 — Make — M Checkout M — Be made for — 1 Book (resolving Student M — Borrow — N Book; both original sides optional). Publisher 1 — Publish — M Book: each book exactly one publisher (mandatory, 1); each publisher one or more books (mandatory, M). Checkout dates are attributes of the associative entity Checkout.

(2)
CREATE TABLE STUDENT (SID CHAR(5) PRIMARY KEY, SNAME VARCHAR(20));
CREATE TABLE PUBLISHER (PID INT PRIMARY KEY, PNAME VARCHAR(30));
CREATE TABLE BOOK (BID INT PRIMARY KEY, TITLE VARCHAR(50), PID INT NOT NULL,
  FOREIGN KEY (PID) REFERENCES PUBLISHER(PID));
CREATE TABLE CHECKOUT (SID CHAR(5), BID INT, CDATE DATE, DDATE DATE, RDATE DATE,
  PRIMARY KEY (SID, BID, CDATE),
  FOREIGN KEY (SID) REFERENCES STUDENT(SID),
  FOREIGN KEY (BID) REFERENCES BOOK(BID));

Key points: CDATE is part of the key because the same student can borrow the same book again; RDATE may be NULL until returned; the FK for the 1:M relationship goes in BOOK.

